:"Morgantown has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Morgantown's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Morgantown has set <strong class=\"sig\">17 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 71 that chance alone would give, and <strong>70 record highs</strong>, close to the 71 that chance alone would give. The 1890s hold the most record highs of any decade (56). Morgantown's all-time high is 105\u00b0F, on August 26, 1893.","t_chg":"Morgantown's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1984.","p_chg":"Across 121 complete years: Morgantown's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.08\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.14), about +1.2\u00b0F since 1874. Morgantown's average high shows no clear change: +0.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.05 to +0.09) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1984, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.75\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.52 to +0.99) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.69\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.36 to +1.02). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 7 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; January fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Morgantown's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.07 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 43.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2003 (60.91 in); the driest, 1930 (25.15 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}